Bean Germination: Structural Observation
Overall Transformation
A dormant, compact unit — the seed — unfolds into a multi-part, spatially distributed organism. The process moves from a single bounded object with internal potential toward an open, branching architecture actively exchanging with its environment. The endpoint looks almost nothing like the starting point, yet every structure at Day 10 traces directly back to structures present or implied on Day 1.

Phases and Transitions
Three fairly clean phases emerge:
Activation (Days 1–2). Nothing visibly differentiates, but the system crosses a threshold — water uptake triggers internal chemistry. This is preparation without apparent external form change. The seed goes from inert to committed.
Axis establishment (Days 3–4). A single polarity appears: root down, shoot up. This is the most decisive structural moment. The plant is now a directed line in space. Two endpoints exist where before there were none, and they are moving in opposite directions.
Elaboration (Days 5–10). The single axis ramifies. Leaves, side roots, and branches appear not randomly but at nodes along existing structure. New growth is always an extension or fork of what already exists — nothing entirely new in kind appears, only multiplications of the patterns set in Phase 2.
The transition between Phase 1 and Phase 2 has a threshold quality — it appears relatively abrupt. The transition between Phase 2 and Phase 3 is gradual, more of a continuous unfolding.

Persistent Structures
The original root tip never disappears — it just moves deeper and spawns side roots. The primary shoot axis, established on Day 3–4, remains the central organizing line through Day 10 and beyond. The first structures are not replaced; they are added to. This is accumulative, not substitutive.
The bilateral/radial symmetry hinted at in the seed appears to persist and elaborate — leaves appear in pairs or alternating nodes, roots branch symmetrically. The pattern established early constrains the shape of everything that follows.

Dissipation and Reset Events
The seed coat is discarded — it’s the one structure that doesn’t persist. It served as boundary and protection during Phase 1 and is shed at or near the Phase 2 transition. This looks like a genuine release event, a one-way door.
There’s no reset. Nothing in this process cycles back to an earlier state. The directionality is strict.

Conclusion
Yes — this is a clear, repeatable pattern, and notably it has a self-similar quality: the branching logic used by roots is the same logic used by shoots. A rule that says “extend a primary axis, then fork it” applies at multiple scales and in both directions. The process also has a strong threshold-then-ramp structure: slow/hidden activation, then a decisive axis-setting moment, then continuous elaboration along that axis. If you showed someone only Days 3–4 and Days 7–10, they could reasonably predict the intermediate steps and extrapolate what came before — the pattern is coherent enough to be legible mid-process.
What’s not present is any return to origin, any oscillation, or any point where accumulated structure is discarded and rebuilt. This is a one-pass unfolding, not a cycle.​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​

